  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive overview of **preventive medicine practices**, organized by the three main levels of prevention:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udd35 What Is Preventive Medicine?\n\nPreventive medicine is a holistic approach to healthcare that considers the whole patient and the myriad factors that influence their well-being. This medical specialty differs from traditional medicine in that its focus is **proactive rather than reactive**.\n\nThere are **3 main types** of preventive medicine: primary, secondary, and tertiary.\n\n---\n\n## 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Primary Prevention\n*(Preventing disease before it occurs)*\n\nPrimary prevention can be defined as an action or behavior that does not allow a disease or adverse event to occur. Examples include **immunization, smoking cessation**, and initiation of an **exercise program** with the goal of disease prevention.\n\nKey practices include:\n\n- **Vaccinations:** Vaccinations represent perhaps the single most effective preventive care measure in medical history. They have eradicated or dramatically reduced many diseases that once caused widespread suffering and death. Vaccines are important in building a child's immune system. Children must have shots against diseases like **mumps and polio**.\n\n- **Lifestyle Counseling:** Clinical preventive medicine physicians may provide **counseling for unhealthy habits**, run preventive health screenings, and administer immunizations. These practitioners may work with patients who would benefit from lifestyle changes, often encountering cases involving diabetes, smoking, or obesity.\n\n- **STI Prevention:** All STIs can be prevented, all can be effectively managed, and many can be cured. Due to new STI screening techniques and technologies, healthcare professionals are now better equipped than ever to help prevent the spread of STIs.\n\n---\n\n## 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Secondary Prevention\n*(Early detection of disease)*\n\nSecondary preventive medicine focuses on the **early detection** of illness, giving the patient a better chance of getting treatment. Detecting the ailment early slows down its progression. Examples of treatments under this category are **newborn screening and cancer screening**.\n\nKey practices include:\n\n- **Cancer Screenings:** Advances in cancer detection led to improved screening for various malignancies; examples include the widespread use of **colonoscopy** in colorectal cancer screening, **mammography** in breast cancer screening, and **Pap smear and human papillomavirus (HPV) testing** in cervical cancer.\n\n- **Cardiovascular Screenings:** Screenings for **elevated cholesterol levels** and **hypertension** serve critical roles in preventing, identifying, and controlling cardiovascular diseases.\n\n- **Routine Measurements:** Recommended preventive practices include **blood pressure, weight and height measurements**, screening for dyslipidemia, screening for alcohol use, screening for smoking, colon and prostate cancer screening, and **influenza immunization**.\n\n- **Well-Child Visits:** Well-child visits provide an opportunity for a medical professional to examine a child holistically, assess their physical and emotional needs, support their growth and development, detect health issues early, and intervene quickly if necessary.\n\n---\n\n## 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Tertiary Prevention\n*(Limiting the damage of an existing illness)*\n\nTertiary preventive medicine aims to **limit the damage** of a current illness. Making the patient as comfortable as possible is part of this level of care. Future care and long-term treatment modalities can help the patient achieve health stability, helping the patient return to daily routines.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udfe5 Clinical vs. Non-Clinical Settings\n\nPreventive medicine can be practiced in both **clinical and non-clinical settings**. Clinical preventive medicine physicians work directly with patients, providing counseling for unhealthy habits, running preventive health screenings, and administering immunizations.\n\nNon-clinical preventive medicine physicians don't work as closely with individual patients. This branch includes **health policy, epidemiology**, and an increased focus on the social and behavioral influences on a person's health.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udca1 Why It Matters\n\nPracticing preventive medicine can lower costs, as **75% of the country's annual health spending** goes toward chronic and largely preventable diseases, according to the CDC. Preventive medicine helps you reduce the risk of illness and lower your healthcare costs.",
